{"task_id": "HumanEval/25", "solution": "def factorize(n: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" Return list of prime factors of given integer in the order from smallest to largest.\n    Each of the factors should be listed number of times corresponding to how many times it appeares in factorization.\n    Input number should be equal to the product of all factors\n    >>> factorize(8)\n    [2, 2, 2]\n    >>> factorize(25)\n    [5, 5]\n    >>> factorize(70)\n    [2, 5, 7]\n    \"\"\"\n    factors = []\n\n    # Handle factor 2\n    while n % 2 == 0:\n        factors.append(2)\n        n //= 2\n\n    # Handle odd factors\n    d = 3\n    while d * d <= n:\n        while n % d == 0:\n            factors.append(d)\n            n //= d\n        d += 2\n\n    # If n is still greater than 1, it must be a prime factor itself\n    if n > 1:\n        factors.append(n)\n\n    return factors"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/25", "solution": "def factorize(n: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" Return list of prime factors of given integer in the order from smallest to largest.\n    Each of the factors should be listed number of times corresponding to how many times it appeares in factorization.\n    \"\"\"\n    factors = []\n    d = 2\n    temp_n = n\n\n    # Handle factors of 2\n    while temp_n % d == 0:\n        factors.append(d)\n        temp_n //= d\n\n    # Handle odd factors\n    d = 3\n    while d * d <= temp_n:\n        while temp_n % d == 0:\n            factors.append(d)\n            temp_n //= d\n        d += 2\n\n    # If temp_n is still greater than 1, it must be a prime number itself\n    if temp_n > 1:\n        factors.append(temp_n)\n\n    return factors"}
